### What is the problem?

Print all even integers between 1 and N inclusive. For N = 10, output: 2 4 6 8 10.

### How do you implement the stepping approach?

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class EvenLister {

    public static List<Integer> evensUpTo(int n) {
        List<Integer> evens = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i += 2) {
            evens.add(i);
        }
        return evens;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Evens up to 10: " + evensUpTo(10));
        System.out.println("Evens up to 1:  " + evensUpTo(1));
    }
}
```

```text
Evens up to 10: [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]
Evens up to 1:  []
```

### How does the filter approach compare?

```java
public static List<Integer> evensFilter(int n) {
    List<Integer> evens = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
        if (i % 2 == 0) evens.add(i);
    }
    return evens;
}
```

| Feature | Stepping | Filter |
|---|---|---|
| Iterations | N/2 | N |
| Per-iteration test | none | modulo |
| Flexibility | fixed stride only | any predicate |
| Readability | very clear | general pattern |

### What are edge cases?

- N = 0 or N = 1: no even numbers, empty result.
- N = 2: single even number [2].
- N is even: N itself is included.
- N is odd: the largest even is N-1.
- Negative N: no even numbers between 1 and a negative number, empty result.

### Why return a list instead of printing?

Returning a list makes the method unit-testable. A test can assert the exact contents. Printing is a side effect that belongs at the call site.

```java
assertEquals(List.of(2, 4, 6, 8, 10), EvenLister.evensUpTo(10));
assertEquals(List.of(), EvenLister.evensUpTo(1));
```

### What about Java Streams?

The `IntStream.iterate` method with a stepping stride mirrors the loop approach directly. The `filter` variant mirrors the filter approach.

```java
import java.util.stream.IntStream;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

// Stepping with IntStream.iterate (Java 9+)
List<Integer> evens = IntStream.iterate(2, i -> i <= 10, i -> i + 2)
    .boxed()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
// [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]

// Filter with IntStream.rangeClosed
List<Integer> evens2 = IntStream.rangeClosed(1, 10)
    .filter(i -> i % 2 == 0)
    .boxed()
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
// [2, 4, 6, 8, 10]
```

### How is this used in a banking batch?

At National Bank of India, even-numbered accounts are assigned to batch A and odd-numbered ones to batch B for overnight reconciliation. The EvenLister produces the worklist for batch A.

```java
List<Integer> batchA = EvenLister.evensUpTo(5000);
System.out.println("Batch A accounts: " + batchA.size()); // 2500
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// Wrong: starting at 1 and stepping by 2 (generates odds!)
for (int i = 1; i <= n; i += 2) // 1, 3, 5, 7, 9

// Correct: start at 2 for evens
for (int i = 2; i <= n; i += 2) // 2, 4, 6, 8, 10
```

### What about performance for very large N?

For very large N, materializing the entire list may consume too much memory. Use `IntStream.iterate(2, i -> i <= n, i -> i + 2)` and process elements lazily with `forEach` instead of collecting. This keeps memory at O(1) while still visiting all even numbers.

### What should a senior engineer know?

The stepping loop is the same pattern behind many stride-based algorithms: even numbers (stride 2), every 5th element (stride 5), or sampling every 100th record. A senior engineer generalizes the pattern and parameterizes the stride. In a banking audit worklist at National Bank of India, even-numbered accounts were selected up to a cutoff, and the list was passed to the next processing stage.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- How would you print odd numbers instead? (Start at 1, step by 2.)
- How would you sum the even numbers instead of listing them?
- What is the closed-form sum of evens from 2 to N?
